# Human in the loop

A **human in the loop** is a person placed at a step of an otherwise
mechanical cycle, where the person interprets a result and decides what the
system does next. In this vault the phrase has a sharper origin: it was
proposed as the ingredient that turns static self-reference into a loop that
moves, by letting someone add axioms to a formal system from outside it.

## Description

### The thesis: the human makes the loop essential

The two rigorous examples of self-description in the discussion, the
[[godel-sentence]] and the [[constructible-universe]], are both static.
Neither modifies itself, and neither involves feedback over time. Granting
that, the proposal was to add a human. A mathematician looks at a theory, sees
that it leaves a statement undecided, and adds a new axiom. The formalism
grows, and the human acts as the "thinking machine" that the formalism lacks.
On this view the human is what makes the definition of a [[strange-loop]]
essential and not merely formal.

Written out, the human's contribution is a [[reflection-progression]]: a chain
of theories in which each extends the one before by a statement the earlier
one could not decide. More interesting still, the human may change not only
the axioms but the criteria by which axioms are chosen.

### The correction: a human is not mathematically required

The reply accepted the construction and rejected the necessity. A program can
carry out a progression of theories. Nothing in the mathematics asks for a
person. What the human supplies is a judgement that is not derivable inside
the current theory, and that judgement can be wrong: adding an undecided
statement does not automatically preserve truth or consistency. Whether human
intelligence exceeds what an algorithm can do here is a philosophical and
scientific question, and not a corollary of the [[incompleteness-theorems]].

So the role survives and the claim is weakened. The loop needs a step taken
from outside the current formalism. A human is one way to fill it, and a
[[reflective-agent]] is another.

### The engineering role: an approval gate

In the design for executable documents the human reappears with a narrower
job. Evaluation of a card produces output, and the output may itself contain
new cells. Two decisions are reserved, for a person or for an agent acting
under review.

- **Integration.** Whether generated content becomes part of the versioned
  knowledge, or stays as inert output.
- **Execution.** Whether any cell in that content is run.

Changes enter as revisions or patches, never as silent self-execution. This
is the separation described in [[render-integrate-execute]], and it is the
difference between a system that can be stopped and one that cannot.

The essay on AI-oriented languages states the same requirement from the
language side: an environment in which a human can "inspect, interrupt,
understand, and redirect" the process. It also moves the human's attention.
Where a checker verifies proofs mechanically, a person no longer reads every
generated line and reviews the propositions instead: what was asked for, and
not how it was done.

## Relations

- It was proposed as what makes a [[strange-loop]] {relation: part-of} essential, a claim the discussion then weakened.
- Its formal form is the [[reflection-progression]] {relation: uses}, where the human chooses each new axiom.
- It is one filler of a role that a [[reflective-agent]] {relation: contrasts} can also fill.
- It stands at the interpretation step of the [[hmd-feedback-loop]] {relation: part-of}.
- It holds the approval gates of [[render-integrate-execute]] {relation: part-of}.
- Its typical operations are [[evaluation]] {relation: uses} of a result and [[revision]] {relation: uses} of the source.
- It is one end of an ordinary [[feedback-loop]] {relation: example-of} unless levels are crossed.
- It bears on the question [[building-a-loop-in-hmd]] {relation: answers}: the loop is to be run "with human feedback".

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[reflection-progression]].** A sequence of theories, each extending the
  last by an axiom it could not decide. The human in the loop is whoever, or
  whatever, picks the axiom.
- **[[reflective-agent]].** A system that uses a model of itself to revise
  its own rules. It is the non-human candidate for the same seat.
- **[[render-integrate-execute]].** Three operations kept apart so that
  generated content cannot run itself. The gaps between them are where the
  human decides.
- **[[hmd-feedback-loop]].** Card, cell, output, interpretation, revision.
  The interpretation step is the one a human occupies.

